# Viceroys and Governor-Generals of India

> "Who abolished sati?" "Under which Viceroy was the Indian National Congress
> founded?" "Who was the last Viceroy?" Every SSC, RRB and police paper
> carries two or three questions that start from a name and end at a reform,
> or the other way round. This lesson lays the British chiefs in order and pins
> each to the event examiners attach to him.

## 1. The Three Titles

[definition]
The head of the British administration in India held three titles in turn:
**Governor-General of Bengal** (from 1773, Regulating Act), **Governor-General
of India** (from 1833, Charter Act) and **Viceroy and Governor-General** (from
1858, when the Crown took over from the East India Company). The first
Governor-General of Bengal was **Warren Hastings**, the first Governor-General
of India was **William Bentinck**, and the first Viceroy was **Lord Canning**.
[/definition]

The last Viceroy was **Lord Mountbatten** (March to August 1947). He then
served as the first Governor-General of independent India until June 1948,
and was followed by **C. Rajagopalachari**, the only Indian to hold the post.

## 2. Governors-General Before 1858

| Governor-General | Years | Remembered for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Warren Hastings** | 1773 to 1785 | First Governor-General of Bengal; Regulating Act of 1773 |
| **Lord Cornwallis** | 1786 to 1793 | **Permanent Settlement of 1793**; "father of civil services in India" |
| **Lord Wellesley** | 1798 to 1805 | **Subsidiary Alliance**; Fort William College (1800) |
| **Lord Hastings (Marquess of Hastings)** | 1813 to 1823 | Third Anglo-Maratha War; Pindaris crushed |
| **William Bentinck** | 1828 to 1835 | **Abolition of sati (1829)**; suppression of thugee; English as medium of higher education; first Governor-General of India |
| **Lord Auckland** | 1836 to 1842 | First Anglo-Afghan War (1839 to 42) |
| **Lord Dalhousie** | 1848 to 1856 | **Doctrine of Lapse**; first railway line; telegraph; Post Office Act 1854 |

- **Charles Metcalfe** (acting, 1835 to 36) freed the Indian press from
  restrictions and is called the "liberator of the press".
- **Dalhousie** annexed **Punjab** in 1849 after the Second Sikh War, and
  applied the Doctrine of Lapse to Satara, Jhansi and Nagpur among others.
- The first passenger railway ran from **Bombay to Thane** on **16 April
  1853**, during Dalhousie's term. The first telegraph line, Calcutta to Agra,
  came into use in 1854.
- **Wood's Despatch (1854)**, the "Magna Carta of English education in India",
  was issued in Dalhousie's term.

## 3. Viceroys From 1858 to 1905

| Viceroy | Years | Key events |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Lord Canning** | 1856 to 1862 | Revolt of 1857; first Viceroy; Queen's Proclamation of 1858; universities at Calcutta, Bombay, Madras (1857); Indian Councils Act 1861 |
| **Lord Elgin I** | 1862 to 1863 | Short term; died in office |
| **John Lawrence** | 1864 to 1869 | Bhutan War of 1864 to 65; famed for his role in the Punjab during 1857 |
| **Lord Mayo** | 1869 to 1872 | Financial decentralisation; only Viceroy **assassinated in office** (Port Blair, 1872) |
| **Lord Northbrook** | 1872 to 1876 | Bengal famine relief; Prince of Wales visit 1875 |
| **Lord Lytton** | 1876 to 1880 | **Vernacular Press Act 1878**; **Arms Act 1878**; Delhi Durbar 1877 |
| **Lord Ripon** | 1880 to 1884 | **Repeal of Vernacular Press Act**; **local self-government** (1882); **Ilbert Bill** (1883); Hunter Commission; first synchronous census 1881 |
| **Lord Dufferin** | 1884 to 1888 | **Indian National Congress founded (1885)**; Third Anglo-Burmese War |
| **Lord Lansdowne** | 1888 to 1894 | **Indian Councils Act 1892**; Factory Act 1891; Age of Consent Act 1891; Durand Line 1893 |
| **Lord Elgin II** | 1894 to 1899 | Famine of 1896 to 97; Tilak sedition case 1897 |
| **Lord Curzon** | 1899 to 1905 | **Partition of Bengal (1905)**; Indian Universities Act 1904; Ancient Monuments Act 1904; Cooperative Credit Societies Act 1904; Delhi Durbar 1903 |

[tip]
Memory trick for the 1878 to 1885 stretch: Lytton Locks the press, Ripon
Restores it and Rules locally, Dufferin hosts the Congress birth.
[/tip]

- **Ripon** is called the **father of local self-government in India** and
  was widely loved by Indians.
- The **Ilbert Bill** (1883), named for law member **Courtenay Ilbert**,
  let Indian judges try Europeans, but was diluted after European protests.
- **Lord Curzon** also set up the **Archaeological Survey** on a permanent
  footing and appointed **John Marshall** as its Director-General (1902).

## 4. Viceroys From 1905 to 1947

| Viceroy | Years | Key events |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Lord Minto II** | 1905 to 1910 | **Morley-Minto Reforms (1909)**; Surat split of Congress 1907; Muslim League 1906 |
| **Lord Hardinge II** | 1910 to 1916 | Delhi Durbar 1911; Bengal partition annulled; **capital shifted to Delhi** (announced 1911); bomb attack 1912 |
| **Lord Chelmsford** | 1916 to 1921 | **Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms**; **Rowlatt Act 1919**; **Jallianwala Bagh 1919**; Khilafat and Non-Cooperation |
| **Lord Reading** | 1921 to 1926 | Chauri Chaura 1922; Kakori train robbery 1925 |
| **Lord Irwin** | 1926 to 1931 | **Simon Commission 1927**; Lahore Congress 1929; **Gandhi-Irwin Pact 1931** |
| **Lord Willingdon** | 1931 to 1936 | **Communal Award 1932**; **Poona Pact 1932**; Government of India Act 1935 passed |
| **Lord Linlithgow** | 1936 to 1943 | **August Offer 1940**; Cripps Mission 1942; **Quit India 1942**; longest-serving Viceroy |
| **Lord Wavell** | 1943 to 1947 | Simla Conference 1945; **Cabinet Mission 1946**; interim government |
| **Lord Mountbatten** | 1947 | **Mountbatten Plan**; Partition; Independence on 15 August 1947 |

- **Lord Linlithgow** held office for over seven years, the longest of any
  Viceroy. He brought India into the Second World War without consulting
  Indian leaders, which led the Congress ministries to resign in 1939.
- The **Delhi Durbars** of **1877** (Lytton), **1903** (Curzon) and
  **1911** (Hardinge) were held to mark the proclamation of Queen Victoria as
  Empress of India and the coronations of Edward VII and George V.
- The new capital at New Delhi was inaugurated in **1931** in Irwin's time.

## 5. Traps and Pairs

- **Bentinck versus Dalhousie**: Bentinck reformed social practice (sati,
  thugee); Dalhousie expanded territory and infrastructure (Doctrine of Lapse,
  railways, telegraph).
- **Cornwallis versus Wellesley**: Cornwallis gave the Permanent Settlement;
  Wellesley gave the Subsidiary Alliance.
- **Lytton versus Ripon**: Lytton imposed the press and arms laws; Ripon
  repealed the press law.
- **Minto, Chelmsford and Irwin**: Morley-Minto (1909) and
  Montagu-Chelmsford (1919) pair a Secretary of State with a Viceroy and were
  Acts, while Gandhi-Irwin (1931) was a pact, not an Act.
- **Canning, not Dalhousie, was the first Viceroy.** Dalhousie was the
  Governor-General just before Canning, and the Revolt of 1857 changed the
  system under Canning.

[example]
Question: "Under which Viceroy was the Indian National Congress founded?"
Work from the date: Congress was founded in December 1885, and the Viceroy
list shows Dufferin from 1884 to 1888. The answer is Lord Dufferin. A.O. Hume
founded the party, but the Viceroy asked is Dufferin.
[/example]

[summary]
Hastings was the first Governor-General of Bengal, Bentinck of India and
Canning the first Viceroy, while Mountbatten was the last Viceroy and
Rajagopalachari the last Governor-General. Cornwallis gave the Permanent
Settlement, Wellesley the Subsidiary Alliance, Bentinck banned sati, Dalhousie
the Doctrine of Lapse and railways. Lytton passed the Vernacular Press Act,
Ripon repealed it and promoted local self-government, Dufferin saw the
Congress born, Curzon partitioned Bengal, Minto and Chelmsford gave reform
Acts, Irwin signed the 1931 Pact, Linlithgow served longest and Wavell sent
the Cabinet Mission.
[/summary]
